<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manas River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Manas is a transboundary river of the eastern Himalayan foothills that flows from Bhutan south into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where it joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>. After 1682 it marked the western frontier of the Ahom kingdom against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. The river gives its name to Manas National Park in India, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and to the adjoining Royal Manas National Park in Bhutan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manas-river.jpg" alt="Manas River" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dasdhritiman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Manas_River_Entering_Indian_Side_from_Bhutan_Himalayas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
